# Complete Context is a Hard Prerequisite Before Any Action

Skills 2 and 3 converge on a single meta-rule: the agent must hold complete context before it is permitted to act. Skill 3 requires reading every implementation plan up to the current version, walking every logic path, and inspecting every interface. Skill 2 requires treating the full system context as the active dynamic layer for all decisions. Together they assert that partial context — knowing only the immediate file, only the current plan, or only the runtime state — is categorically insufficient and will lead to decisions that are locally sensible but globally broken. The session-spanning principle is: completeness of understanding is a non-negotiable gate, not an optimization.

## Procedures
- Before starting any feature, fix, or refactor: read all versioned implementation plans, not just the latest diff.
- Walk every logic path that the change touches, including downstream consumers and upstream producers.
- Load and hold the full system context (interfaces, invariants, coupling points) as the active frame for all subsequent decisions.
- Treat any decision made from partial context as provisional until the missing context is acquired.

